---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const fullConfig = `import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import lockxy from '@wesflo/vite-plugin-lockxy';

export default defineConfig(({ command }) => ({
  plugins: command === 'serve' ? [
    lockxy({
      mockRoot: new URL('./mock/', import.meta.url),
      requestPrefixes: ['/api/'],
      extensions: ['.xml'],
      contentTypes: {
        '.xml': 'application/xml; charset=utf-8',
      },
      manifestFileName: 'mock.manifest',
      debug: false,
      logging: true,
    }),
  ] : [],
}));`;

const multiplePrefixesConfig = `import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import lockxy from '@wesflo/vite-plugin-lockxy';

export default defineConfig(({ command }) => ({
  plugins: command === 'serve' ? [
    lockxy({
      requestPrefixes: ['/api/', '/development-api/'],
    }),
  ] : [],
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/development-api': {
        target: 'https://api.v1.supertoll.domain.de',
        changeOrigin: true,
        rewrite: (path) => path.replace(/^\\/development-api/, ''),
      },
    },
  },
}));`;

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<DocsLayout title="Vite Plugin" description="Configure Lockxy's Vite plugin and file resolution." activePath="/vite-plugin/">
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Convention before configuration</p>
        <h1>Configure the Vite plugin.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Lockxy intercepts matching requests in Vite's development server and resolves local files by URL and method.
            The defaults are enough for most projects; every option below is optional.
        </p>

        <h2 id="file-naming-conventions">File naming conventions</h2>
        <p>
            The matched request prefix is removed and the remaining URL becomes a path below <code>mockRoot</code>. For
            every candidate, the uppercased method-specific filename is checked before the generic filename.
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Preferred file</th><th>Generic fallback</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/GET_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/POST_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/reports/monthly</code></td><td><code>reports/GET_monthly.pdf</code></td><td><code>reports/monthly.pdf</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            More specific route candidates win before suffix and parent fallbacks. Looking only at JSON,
            <code>PUT /api/users/profile</code> checks <code>users/PUT_profile.json</code>,
            <code>users/profile.json</code>, <code>PUT_profile.json</code>, and <code>profile.json</code> in that order.
            The same process is repeated in the configured extension order.
        </p>

        <h2 id="plugin-options">Plugin options</h2>
        <Code code={fullConfig} label="vite.config.ts" />
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>mockRoot</code></td><td><code>URL</code></td><td><code>&lt;Vite root&gt;/mock/</code></td><td>Directory containing mock files and the manifest.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>requestPrefixes</code></td><td><code>string | readonly string[]</code></td><td><code>['/api/']</code></td><td>One or more local URL namespaces handled by Lockxy.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>extensions</code></td><td><code>readonly string[]</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>Additional file extensions appended to the built-in candidates.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>contentTypes</code></td><td><code>Readonly&lt;Record&lt;string, string&gt;&gt;</code></td><td><code>{}</code></td><td>Additional or overridden content types by extension.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>manifestFileName</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>mock.manifest</code></td><td>Manifest basename or explicit <code>.json</code>, <code>.yaml</code>, or <code>.yml</code> filename inside <code>mockRoot</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Enables detailed manifest and referenced-file validation.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>logging</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Logs method, URL, delay, status, source, and runtime errors.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            Additional extensions are appended after JSON, PDF, CSV, text, JPEG, PNG, and WebP. Unknown extensions use
            <code>application/octet-stream</code> unless a matching <code>contentTypes</code> entry is configured.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Fixed manifest route">
            <p>
                The development-only <code>/_lockxy/manifest</code> route is intentionally fixed and cannot be
                overridden through plugin options. Every response produced by Lockxy includes
                <code>x-lockxy: true</code>, so mocked traffic is immediately recognizable in the browser network panel.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="multiple-request-prefixes">Multiple request prefixes and development APIs</h3>
        <p>
            Pass a string for one namespace or an array for several. If prefixes overlap, the longest matching value wins
            regardless of configuration order.
        </p>
        <Code code={multiplePrefixesConfig} label="vite.config.ts" />
        <p>
            Lockxy runs before Vite's proxy. A matching local mock is returned immediately; an explicitly bypassed request
            continues to the configured development API. This local prefix approach also supports remote domains with any
            subdomain depth without teaching the mock resolver about external hosts.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Missing mocks stay visible">
            <p>
                A matching prefix without a local file returns Lockxy's <code>404</code>. It does not silently fall through
                to the remote API. Use endpoint or global bypass when passthrough is intentional.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="runtime-file-cache">Runtime file index and manifest cache</h3>
        <p>
            Lockxy recursively indexes paths below <code>mockRoot</code> and loads the optional manifest once when Vite
            starts. Candidate resolution checks this in-memory index and reads only the selected response file instead of
            probing every possible filename on disk.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>The mock directory is watched even when it is outside <code>src</code>.</li>
            <li>Adding or removing files rebuilds the path index.</li>
            <li>Creating, replacing, or editing the manifest reloads its cached configuration.</li>
            <li>Response contents are not cached, so file edits are visible immediately.</li>
            <li>All runtime indexes start from scratch with each Vite server start.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="optional-manifest">Next: optional response behavior</h2>
        <p>
            Plugin options define where and how Lockxy resolves requests. Use the separate
            <a href={`${base}/manifest/`}>Manifest guide</a> when selected calls need statuses, delays, explicit files,
            dynamic paths, or multiple scenarios. A manifest remains completely optional.
        </p>

        <Callout title="Development only">
            <p>
                Include Lockxy only for Vite's serve command. The plugin refuses to register mock middleware for a
                production build or production mode and prints a prominent warning. Mock files should contain synthetic
                data only.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
